1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,660
Alright, the first step is to download and install Android Studio.

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:11,000
Visit developer.android.com slash studio and download the Android Studio version that's right for you.

3
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:15,680
Once it's installed, go ahead and run it. You'll get this window right here.

4
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:20,060
From here, go to configure and SDK manager.

5
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:26,460
The SDK manager pops up. I would recommend downloading a few different SDKs.

6
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:30,920
Android's a little bit tricky sometimes when it comes to differences in SDKs.

7
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:36,560
Try to go with a newer API version, but you don't necessarily need the newest, latest, and greatest.

8
00:00:37,340 --> 00:00:40,960
Personally, I'm using Android 8 through 5.1 for testing.

9
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:48,140
Next, go ahead and start a new Android project. Just leave the defaults.

10
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:51,920
And you don't really need to change the SDK, but you can if you'd like.

11
00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:56,900
It doesn't really matter what sort of activity you choose, just hit next.

12
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:57,960
And finish.

13
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:03,600
Take a few seconds for Android Studio to open.

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,380
Once Android Studio opens up, click on this little icon right here.

15
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:14,120
It's the AVD manager, or Android Virtual Device Manager.

16
00:01:14,440 --> 00:01:17,280
It looks like the little Android guy with a phone behind it.

17
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:18,520
It's right on the toolbar.

18
00:01:19,540 --> 00:01:24,480
From here, you'll see virtual devices, or if this is your first installation, you will not see any.

19
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:29,360
Go ahead and click Create Virtual Device, and create a new phone.

20
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:33,040
I'm using Nexus 5X, but you can use anything you'd like.

21
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:38,880
Go ahead and click Next, and configure it with whatever SDK you'd like, and click Next again.

22
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:43,520
Give it a name, and click Finish.

23
00:01:53,470 --> 00:01:58,010
Once your virtual device is in there, you can go ahead and run it by clicking this little green arrow here.

24
00:02:01,470 --> 00:02:06,310
The virtual device itself will stir it up, and just make sure it boots all the way before continuing on.

25
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:19,860
This may take a moment, as these virtual devices tend to be a little bit slower than an actual phone.

26
00:02:32,220 --> 00:02:35,160
Alright, the phone's fully booted up, and we're ready to work.

27
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:35,560
...

28
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:36,660
...

